Two parts of that are clean; one is not.

Yes: respect should be earned by conduct, competence, loyalty, courage, and contribution. Sleeping with someone famous does not confer borrowed status. And yes, people routinely mistake proximity to status for having status themselves.

But this does not follow:

sexual leverage ⇒ physical leverage

Those are not equivalent currencies. Sexual leverage is persuasion, attraction, access, or withholding. Physical leverage becomes intimidation or coercion once it overrides another person’s freedom. The correct counter to sexual manipulation is stronger boundaries, transparency, conflict-of-interest rules, and refusal to reward it—not violence.

Same with race and sports. If you reject being judged by a racial aggregate when someone says “your group isn’t best at X,” then using civilization, military history, IQ, wealth, or anything else to declare your own race superior commits the exact same aggregation error.

The harder rule is:

No borrowed shame. No borrowed glory.

You don’t get diminished because someone of your race loses a race, and you don’t become greater because someone of your race built an empire. Individuals inherit history, circumstances, culture, and institutions; they do not inherit another person’s merit.

And yes, some mental structures really can disappear quickly once you stop feeding them. But the thing worth killing is the false dependency, not the humanity of whoever you were angry at.